WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.740
I want to begin by saying that this video contains absolutely no spoilers whatsoever.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:08.540
Well, except in so far as just being alive is a minor spoiler,

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.480
on account of how you're connected to every other person on earth,

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.540
a growing number of whom have already seen the Star Wars movie.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.040
And you know they're very slightly different for having had that experience

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:22.440
and their slight differences are causing very slight differences in you and etc.,

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.240
as the Force ripples through the Universe.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.000
Which is actually what I want to talk about today.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:30.280
So Hank, I've now seen the new Star Wars movie twice, and I really really like it,

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:31.640
and I'm trying to figure out why.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:35.720
Alright, this is Joseph Campbell who wrote a famous book about shared mythologies

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:40.120
across cultures and historical eras called The Hero With a Thousand Faces.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.280
Basically, Campbell pointed out that a lot of our most important stories

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.640
all involve a plot where, I'm just gonna quote him:

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:50.280
"... a hero ventures forth from the world of common day into a region of supernatural wonder,

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.920
fabulous forces are there encountered, and a decisive victory is won."

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.120
Of course, it can't be too decisive because, you know, these are movies and they need sequels,

00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:59.400
but you get the idea.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.920
Campbell's writing about shared mythologies and mythological structures

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.560
was extremely influential to George Lucas when he was writing the original Star Wars movies.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:12.120
And it shows. I mean, Luke Skywalker follows the heroic journey that Campbell laid out step by step.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.360
So Hank I think the original Star Wars trilogy is seen as important today

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.620
not just because they're good movies, or because the special effects and music are amazing,

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.340
but because as myth they are brilliant.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.420
Luke Skywalker gave the hero of a thousand faces yet one more face,

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:32.980
and in his story I got to experience that intoxicating mix of awe and recognition that we seek from myth.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:36.680
Right, like, recognition because I too have been a petulant ne'er-do-well

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.680
in a boring hometown harboring a dream of going to space.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.200
And all because Lord Vader and the Death Star were intensely awesome

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.940
in the old fashioned sense of the word that involved fear.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:46.900
But it's also more than that.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:51.500
Like any good myth, Star Wars helps us to understand and identify ourselves as people.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:55.000
Consider for instance how much the assigning and signaling of personhood matters

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.660
in the original Star Wars movies.

00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:01.280
I mean, we feel bad for R2-D2, who is a droid, when he is damaged.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.340
We also deeply empathize with Chewie, even though he is not a human and speaks incomprehensibly.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:10.280
But we don't empathize with the Stormtroopers, even though they're humanoid and speak English,

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.640
because they're not people, they're... actually we didn't know what they were back in 1977.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.540
But we knew that they were not proper people like us and Chewbacca.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:22.780
Now, of course, the original Star Wars movies did get their fair share of criticisms -

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:27.620
people pointed out that the dialogue was hokey, that the galaxy was astonishingly small

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.660
and given over to unlikely coincidence -

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:32.600
but for me, all that stuff was totally irrelevant.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:36.980
I mean, the dialogue in Jonah's encounter with the whale also leaves a bit to be desired,

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:38.940
but that is *so* beside the point.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:43.080
And then the prequels came out, and they did not have the same mythic resonance.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.660
In fact, for many people, they were just... ya know... movies.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.360
And as movies, they weren't particularly great.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.520
Honestly, Hank, after those movies I felt like there couldn't be another good Star Wars movie,

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.640
because Star Wars had just given to the common myth what it had to give.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:01.120
But I was wrong! The Force Awakens is so great!

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:04.180
The Force Awakens honors the myth of the hero's journey from the original Star Wars,

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.580
but it also expands and deepens it.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:10.420
Along the way the movie pushes us toward a broader understanding of personhood,

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:12.000
of what constitutes us.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.340
In fact, Hank, we saw this in the first teaser footage released months before the movie.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:20.720
A Stormtrooper removes his helmet and proves to have a very human face.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:26.020
Those we could dismiss for the first six movies are suddenly, inarguably, people

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:27.540
who are connected to us.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.500
And in the movie itself there's much more.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:33.420
For instance, we see that the hero with a thousand faces need not be male, or white

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.300
or even a petulant ne'er-do-well.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:36.600
It's not a perfect movie, of course.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.540
The galaxy is still astonishingly small, the dialogue still sometimes clunky,

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:42.820
but it's a very effective myth.

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:47.940
Hank, to be honest with you, I've been worried lately that I've grown too old to properly love a movie.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.300
I mean, they're just movies.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:52.740
But I haven't! I can still nerd out about a movie I love!

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:53.440
Yes!

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:55.020
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.500
In the meantime, I'm just gonna say it -

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:58.696
May the Force be with you.

